Q: how to solve: Could not resolve &quot;zone.js/node&quot; A: <p>This can be caused by having a polyfill with an extension. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>            &quot;architect&quot;: {
                &quot;build&quot;: {
                    &quot;builder&quot;: &quot;@angular/build:application&quot;,
                    &quot;options&quot;: {
                        &quot;polyfills&quot;: [
                            &quot;src/polyfills.ts&quot;
                        ],
</code></pre>
<p>See: <a href="https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/issues/34001" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/issues/34001</a></p>

Q: Create delegate for struct instance method without boxing the target A: <p>If you're able to change the signature of the delegate and the consuming API (i.e. you're not passing it into an API that's outside of your control), then there's a workaround of sorts:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Delegate with byref target parameter
delegate void RefTargetAction&lt;TTarget, TArg1&gt;(ref TTarget target, TArg1 arg1);

// Consuming API accepts byref target and delegate
void MyMethod&lt;TTarget&gt;(ref TTarget target, RefTargetAction&lt;TTarget, int&gt; action) {
  action(ref target, 42);
}

// Example usage
var myStruct = new MyMutableStruct(someData);
var myAction = new RefTargetAction&lt;MyStruct, int&gt;((ref MyStruct target, int arg1) =&gt; {
  target.mutableField = arg1;
});
MyMethod(ref myStruct, myAction);
</code></pre>
<p>It's a bit awkward, but you may not need to use generics as much as I did here.</p>
